## Unidirectional @OneToMany (Creates Join Table)

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    // Without mappedBy: creates order_items JOIN TABLE (usually wrong)
    @OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL, fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private List<Item> items = new ArrayList<>();
}
```

## Bidirectional @OneToMany (Correct)

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private BigDecimal total;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order",  // 'order' = field name in Item
               cascade = CascadeType.ALL,
               fetch = FetchType.LAZY,
               orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Item> items = new ArrayList<>();

    // Helper method to keep both sides in sync
    public void addItem(Item item) {
        items.add(item);
        item.setOrder(this);  // Set owning side
    }

    public void removeItem(Item item) {
        items.remove(item);
        item.setOrder(null);
    }
}

@Entity
public class Item {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String name;
    private BigDecimal price;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)  // Owning side — holds FK column
    @JoinColumn(name = "order_id")     // Foreign key column name
    private Order order;
}
```

## Cascade Types

| Cascade | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `PERSIST` | Saving parent also saves children |
| `MERGE` | Merging parent also merges children |
| `REMOVE` | Deleting parent also deletes children |
| `REFRESH` | Refreshing parent also refreshes children |
| `ALL` | All of the above |

`orphanRemoval = true` — removes items from DB when removed from the collection, even without `REMOVE` cascade.

## Performance Warning

```java
// Bad — triggers N+1 on the 'orders' query then batch loads
@OneToMany(fetch = FetchType.EAGER)  // NEVER for collections!
private List<Item> items;

// Good — lazy by default, load explicitly when needed
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
private List<Item> items;
```

## Production Reality

`orphanRemoval = true` is the right choice when the child entity only makes sense as part of the parent — line items in an order, paragraphs in a document. If you remove an item from the order's list, it's deleted from the database. Without `orphanRemoval`, removed items become orphaned (order_id set to null) — almost always wrong.

## @ManyToOne is the Owning Side

```java
@Entity
public class OrderItem {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String productName;
    private int quantity;
    private BigDecimal price;

    // Owning side — order_id FK lives in order_items table
    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)  // Override EAGER default!
    @JoinColumn(name = "order_id", nullable = false)
    private Order order;
}
```

## Default Fetch Type Problem

```java
// Without explicit fetch type: @ManyToOne → EAGER
@ManyToOne
private Order order;  // Loads Order even when you only need item.getPrice()

// Generated SQL:
SELECT i.*, o.* FROM order_items i
INNER JOIN orders o ON i.order_id = o.id
WHERE i.id = ?;
// Order loaded whether you need it or not!

// With LAZY:
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
private Order order;  // Proxy created, Order loaded only on first access

// Generated SQL:
SELECT i.* FROM order_items i WHERE i.id = ?;
// Order NOT loaded until you call item.getOrder().getTotal()
```

## @JoinColumn Options

```java
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@JoinColumn(
    name = "category_id",          // FK column name in this table
    nullable = false,              // NOT NULL constraint
    foreignKey = @ForeignKey(name = "fk_product_category")  // Named FK
)
private Category category;
```

## Composite FK

```java
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@JoinColumns({
    @JoinColumn(name = "company_id", referencedColumnName = "company_id"),
    @JoinColumn(name = "employee_id", referencedColumnName = "employee_id")
})
private Employee employee;
```

## Production Reality

Setting `fetch = FetchType.LAZY` on all `@ManyToOne` associations is a safe default. When you need the associated object, use JOIN FETCH in your JPQL query or an Entity Graph for that specific use case. This gives you explicit control over what's loaded and when, rather than silent eager loading.

## Basic @ManyToMany

```java
@Entity
public class Product {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String name;

    // Owner side — defines the join table
    @ManyToMany(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinTable(
        name = "product_tag",
        joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "product_id"),
        inverseJoinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "tag_id")
    )
    private Set<Tag> tags = new HashSet<>();  // Use Set, not List
}

@Entity
public class Tag {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String name;

    @ManyToMany(mappedBy = "tags", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)  // Inverse side
    private Set<Product> products = new HashSet<>();
}
```

## When Join Table Has Extra Columns — Intermediate Entity

```java
// Can't use @ManyToMany — enrollment has grade and enrollmentDate
@Entity
public class Enrollment {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "student_id")
    private Student student;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "course_id")
    private Course course;

    // Extra columns — impossible with plain @ManyToMany
    private LocalDate enrolledAt;
    private String grade;

    @Version
    private Integer version;
}

// In Student entity:
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "student", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
private Set<Enrollment> enrollments = new HashSet<>();

// In Course entity:
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "course")
private Set<Enrollment> enrollments = new HashSet<>();
```

## List vs Set Issue

```java
// With List — Hibernate deletes ALL and reinserts on any change
@ManyToMany
List<Tag> tags;  // Adding one tag: DELETE all, INSERT all

// With Set — Hibernate makes precise changes
@ManyToMany
Set<Tag> tags;  // Adding one tag: INSERT one row only
```

## Production Reality

In practice, most `@ManyToMany` relationships evolve to need extra columns. Start with an intermediate entity from the beginning to avoid a difficult refactoring later. The extra code is minimal and prevents future headaches.

## Standard @OneToOne

```java
@Entity
public class User {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String email;

    // Inverse side — can't be truly lazy!
    @OneToOne(mappedBy = "user", fetch = FetchType.LAZY, cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    private UserProfile profile;
}

@Entity
public class UserProfile {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String bio;
    private String avatarUrl;

    // Owning side — holds FK column
    @OneToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "user_id", unique = true)  // unique = true enforces 1:1
    private User user;
}
```

## @PrimaryKeyJoinColumn (True Lazy Loading)

```java
@Entity
public class User {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String email;

    @OneToOne(mappedBy = "user", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private UserProfile profile;  // Now truly lazy!
}

@Entity
public class UserProfile {
    @Id private Long id;  // Same ID as User

    @OneToOne
    @PrimaryKeyJoinColumn  // Shared PK — no separate FK column
    private User user;

    @MapsId  // Or use @MapsId to derive ID from parent
    @OneToOne
    private User user;
}
```

## @MapsId (Cleanest Shared PK)

```java
@Entity
public class UserProfile {
    @Id  // Same value as user.id
    private Long userId;

    @OneToOne
    @MapsId  // userId is derived from user's PK
    @JoinColumn(name = "user_id")
    private User user;

    private String bio;
}
```

## Production Reality

If User and UserProfile are always loaded together, merge them into one entity — simpler, no JOIN needed. If UserProfile is rarely needed (loaded only on profile page), keep them separate and use `@PrimaryKeyJoinColumn` or `@MapsId` to enable true lazy loading. The false-LAZY issue with standard `@OneToOne` is a hidden performance trap that many developers discover only through production monitoring.

## How LAZY Works

```java
OrderItem item = itemRepository.findById(1L).orElseThrow();
// SQL: SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE id = 1
// item.order is a CGLIB proxy — no DB hit yet

String orderNum = item.getOrder().getOrderNumber();
// NOW: SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id = 5
// Proxy is initialised on first property access
```

## How EAGER Works

```java
// @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.EAGER)  ← default for @ManyToOne!
OrderItem item = itemRepository.findById(1L).orElseThrow();
// SQL: SELECT i.*, o.* FROM order_items i
//      INNER JOIN orders o ON i.order_id = o.id
//      WHERE i.id = 1
// order is fully loaded whether you need it or not
```

## Default Fetch Types

| Annotation | Default |
|---|---|
| `@OneToMany` | LAZY ✓ |
| `@ManyToMany` | LAZY ✓ |
| `@ManyToOne` | **EAGER** ← change this! |
| `@OneToOne` | **EAGER** ← change this! |

## The Cascade of EAGER

```java
@Entity
public class OrderItem {
    @ManyToOne  // EAGER by default → loads Order
    private Order order;
}

@Entity
public class Order {
    @ManyToOne  // EAGER by default → loads Customer
    private Customer customer;
}

@Entity
public class Customer {
    @ManyToOne  // EAGER by default → loads Address
    private Address address;
}

// Loading one OrderItem triggers: item JOIN order JOIN customer JOIN address
// Loading a page of 20 OrderItems joins 4 tables for all rows
```

## Load Per Use Case with JOIN FETCH

```java
// Load order items list — don't need order details
@Query("SELECT i FROM OrderItem i WHERE i.status = :status")
List<OrderItem> findByStatus(@Param("status") ItemStatus status);

// Load for invoice — need order and customer details
@Query("SELECT i FROM OrderItem i JOIN FETCH i.order o JOIN FETCH o.customer WHERE i.id = :id")
Optional<OrderItem> findByIdForInvoice(@Param("id") Long id);
```

## Production Reality

Audit all your `@ManyToOne` and `@OneToOne` mappings and add `fetch = FetchType.LAZY` explicitly. Enable SQL logging (`show-sql=true`) in development and review every query a simple `findAll()` generates — if you see unexpected JOINs, you have EAGER mappings to fix.

## Cascade Types

| CascadeType | Triggered By | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| `PERSIST` | `em.persist(parent)` | Also persists new children |
| `MERGE` | `em.merge(parent)` | Also merges detached children |
| `REMOVE` | `em.remove(parent)` | Also removes children |
| `REFRESH` | `em.refresh(parent)` | Also refreshes children |
| `DETACH` | `em.detach(parent)` | Also detaches children |
| `ALL` | All of the above | All cascade operations |

## PERSIST + MERGE for Owned Children

```java
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "order",
           cascade = {CascadeType.PERSIST, CascadeType.MERGE},
           fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
private List<OrderItem> items = new ArrayList<>();

// Usage: save order and new items in one operation
Order order = new Order();
order.addItem(new OrderItem("Widget", 2, BigDecimal.valueOf(10.00)));
orderRepository.save(order);  // Cascades PERSIST → items saved too
```

## CascadeType.ALL for Completely Owned Children

```java
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "order",
           cascade = CascadeType.ALL,
           orphanRemoval = true,  // Remove items removed from collection
           fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
private List<OrderItem> items;

// Deleting the order cascades REMOVE → all items deleted
orderRepository.delete(order);  // Also deletes order_items
```

## DANGER: REMOVE on @ManyToMany

```java
@Entity
public class Product {
    // WRONG — CascadeType.REMOVE on ManyToMany!
    @ManyToMany(cascade = CascadeType.REMOVE)  // DON'T DO THIS
    private Set<Tag> tags;
    // Deleting a product REMOVES the tags from the database!
    // Other products lose their tags too!
}

// Correct — no REMOVE cascade on shared entities
@ManyToMany(cascade = {CascadeType.PERSIST, CascadeType.MERGE})
private Set<Tag> tags;
// Deleting a product leaves tags intact
```

## orphanRemoval vs CascadeType.REMOVE

```java
// orphanRemoval: removes items dropped from the collection
order.getItems().remove(item);  // item is deleted from DB on flush

// CascadeType.REMOVE: deletes children when parent is deleted
orderRepository.delete(order);  // all items deleted

// They're complementary — use both for fully owned collections
```

## Production Reality

For `@OneToMany` owned children: `cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true` is the standard pattern. For `@ManyToMany` shared entities: `cascade = {PERSIST, MERGE}` only. Never add `REMOVE` to relationships where the child entity is shared with other parents.

## @Embeddable Value Object

```java
@Embeddable
public class Address {
    @Column(name = "street", nullable = false)
    private String street;

    @Column(name = "city", nullable = false)
    private String city;

    @Column(name = "postal_code", length = 10)
    private String postalCode;

    @Column(name = "country", length = 2)
    private String country;  // ISO 2-letter code

    protected Address() { }  // For Hibernate

    public Address(String street, String city, String postalCode, String country) {
        this.street = street;
        this.city = city;
        this.postalCode = postalCode;
        this.country = country;
    }
}
```

## Using @Embedded

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "orders")
public class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String orderNumber;

    @Embedded
    private Address shippingAddress;  // Columns in orders table
    // shipping_street, shipping_city, shipping_postal_code, shipping_country
    // (uses Address column names by default)
}
```

## Same Type Embedded Twice — @AttributeOverrides

```java
@Entity
public class Customer {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @Embedded
    @AttributeOverrides({
        @AttributeOverride(name = "street", column = @Column(name = "billing_street")),
        @AttributeOverride(name = "city", column = @Column(name = "billing_city")),
        @AttributeOverride(name = "postalCode", column = @Column(name = "billing_postal")),
        @AttributeOverride(name = "country", column = @Column(name = "billing_country"))
    })
    private Address billingAddress;

    @Embedded
    @AttributeOverrides({
        @AttributeOverride(name = "street", column = @Column(name = "shipping_street")),
        @AttributeOverride(name = "city", column = @Column(name = "shipping_city")),
        @AttributeOverride(name = "postalCode", column = @Column(name = "shipping_postal")),
        @AttributeOverride(name = "country", column = @Column(name = "shipping_country"))
    })
    private Address shippingAddress;
}
```

## @Embedded Money Type

```java
@Embeddable
public class Money {
    @Column(name = "amount", precision = 19, scale = 4, nullable = false)
    private BigDecimal amount;

    @Column(name = "currency", length = 3, nullable = false)
    private String currency;  // ISO 4217: USD, EUR, GBP

    // Value object semantics
    public Money add(Money other) {
        if (!this.currency.equals(other.currency)) throw new CurrencyMismatchException();
        return new Money(this.amount.add(other.amount), this.currency);
    }
}

@Entity
public class Order {
    @Embedded
    private Money total;
}
```

## Production Reality

`@Embeddable` is an excellent tool for domain modeling. Instead of twenty flat columns in the entity, group related columns into meaningful value objects. This improves readability, enables value-level operations (like `Money.add()`), and keeps your entity clean. Model richly — domain concepts that always belong together are good candidates for embedding.

## Inconsistent State Problem

```java
// Without helper methods — inconsistent state!
Order order = new Order();
Item item = new Item("Widget", BigDecimal.TEN);

// Set owning side only
item.setOrder(order);
// order.getItems() is EMPTY — inconsistent!

// Or set inverse side only
order.getItems().add(item);
// item.getOrder() is NULL — inconsistent!
// AND: FK won't be written (owning side is item)
```

## Helper Methods — The Correct Pattern

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Item> items = new ArrayList<>();

    // Helper: atomically syncs both sides
    public void addItem(Item item) {
        items.add(item);
        item.setOrder(this);  // Set owning side → FK will be written
    }

    public void removeItem(Item item) {
        items.remove(item);
        item.setOrder(null);
    }
}

// Usage
Order order = new Order();
Item widget = new Item("Widget", BigDecimal.TEN);
order.addItem(widget);  // Both sides synced atomically
// widget.getOrder() == order ✓
// order.getItems().contains(widget) ✓
```

## toString()/equals() Pitfall

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String number;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order")
    private List<Item> items;

    // BAD: causes StackOverflowError if Item.toString() includes order
    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Order{id=" + id + ", items=" + items + "}";  // DON'T
    }

    // GOOD: exclude bidirectional reference
    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Order{id=" + id + ", number='" + number + "'}";
    }

    // GOOD equals/hashCode: use natural ID, not generated
    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof Order)) return false;
        Order other = (Order) o;
        return number != null && number.equals(other.number);  // Business key
    }
}
```

## equals/hashCode for Entities

For JPA entities in collections, base `equals()` and `hashCode()` on a natural business key — not the generated ID (which is null before persist) and not all fields:

```java
@Override
public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) return true;
    if (!(o instanceof Item other)) return false;
    return Objects.equals(sku, other.sku);  // Natural business key
}

@Override
public int hashCode() {
    return Objects.hash(sku);
}
```

## Production Reality

Lombok's `@EqualsAndHashCode` generates hashCode from all fields by default — dangerous for JPA entities. Exclude bidirectional associations explicitly. Using Lombok with JPA requires careful `@ToString(exclude = "order")` and `@EqualsAndHashCode(onlyExplicitlyIncluded = true)` with `@EqualsAndHashCode.Include` on the natural key field.

## @JoinColumn Attributes

```java
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@JoinColumn(
    name = "category_id",              // FK column name
    referencedColumnName = "id",       // PK of referenced table (default)
    nullable = false,                  // NOT NULL constraint
    insertable = true,                 // Part of INSERT — default true
    updatable = true,                  // Part of UPDATE — default true
    foreignKey = @ForeignKey(
        name = "fk_product_category",  // Named constraint
        value = ConstraintMode.CONSTRAINT
    )
)
private Category category;
```

## Referencing Non-PK Column

```java
// Join on a unique column that's not the PK
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@JoinColumn(name = "user_email", referencedColumnName = "email")
private User user;
// order.user_email → user.email (must be UNIQUE)
```

## Read-Only Association

```java
// Join column that's managed elsewhere — read-only here
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@JoinColumn(
    name = "user_id",
    insertable = false,  // Don't include in INSERT
    updatable = false    // Don't include in UPDATE
)
private User user;
// Used alongside a 'userId' field that is insertable/updatable
```

## For @OneToOne

```java
@OneToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@JoinColumn(
    name = "billing_address_id",
    unique = true,  // Enforce 1:1 at DB level
    nullable = true  // Optional relationship
)
private Address billingAddress;
```

## Production Reality

Always name your FK constraints explicitly with `@ForeignKey(name = "fk_...")`. Auto-generated constraint names like `fkr8vhsqm5mwuqvz1rmbfk8t1k8` are meaningless in DB error messages and migration scripts. Use a convention like `fk_tablename_referenced_tablename` or `fk_tablename_columnname`.

## Always Initialize Collections

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    // WRONG — NullPointerException on new Order().getItems().add(...)
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order")
    private List<Item> items;  // null!

    // CORRECT — safe to use immediately
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Item> items = new ArrayList<>();
}
```

## List vs Set Recommendation

```java
// @OneToMany — List is fine (ordered, allows duplicates per item type)
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
private List<OrderItem> items = new ArrayList<>();

// @ManyToMany — ALWAYS use Set
@ManyToMany
@JoinTable(...)
private Set<Tag> tags = new HashSet<>();
// With List: Hibernate deletes ALL rows and reinserts on any change
// With Set: Hibernate makes precise inserts/deletes
```

## @OrderColumn — Preserve Order in DB

```java
@Entity
public class Survey {
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "survey", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    @OrderColumn(name = "question_order")  // Adds order column to child table
    private List<Question> questions = new ArrayList<>();
}

// DB: questions(id, text, survey_id, question_order)
// Hibernate maintains question_order automatically
```

## @OrderBy — Sort in Memory/Query

```java
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "post")
@OrderBy("createdAt DESC")  // JPQL expression — sorted in query
private List<Comment> comments = new ArrayList<>();
```

## @ElementCollection — Simple Value Collections

```java
@Entity
public class Product {
    @ElementCollection
    @CollectionTable(
        name = "product_tags",
        joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "product_id")
    )
    @Column(name = "tag")
    private Set<String> tags = new HashSet<>();  // Simple strings, no entity
}
```

## Production Reality

For `equals()` and `hashCode()` to work correctly in `Set`-based collections, your entity must implement them based on a natural business key. If you only override using the generated `@Id` (which is null before persist), adding a new entity to a `Set` and then persisting it will put a duplicate in the set — the null-id and the set-id are different.
